  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I remove metadata from a PDF for free?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The fastest free method is FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover — it runs entirely in your browser, removes all three metadata layers (Info dictionary, trailer dictionary, and XMP stream), and downloads a clean file. No upload, no account needed. Alternatively, ExifTool (command line, free) can strip all fields with: exiftool -all= document.pdf"
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does saving a PDF remove its metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. Saving or re-exporting a PDF does not remove existing metadata — it typically adds to it. Each time you save in Word, Acrobat, or any PDF editor, the software updates the ModDate field and may add Producer information. To actually remove metadata you need a dedicated stripping tool."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What metadata does a PDF contain?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A PDF typically contains: Author (your full name from your OS or Office account), Creator (the software that created the original document), Producer (the software that generated the PDF), ModDate (last modification timestamp), CreationDate, Company, Manager, Keywords, and Subject. Modern PDFs store this in two separate locations — the Info dictionary and the XMP stream — which is why partial stripping often fails."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does Adobe Acrobat remove all PDF metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Partially. Acrobat Pro's 'Remove Hidden Information' tool clears many fields but does not always fully remove the XMP stream. After using Acrobat's built-in cleaner, running ExifTool on the output often still reveals metadata fields. For complete removal, use a tool specifically designed to strip all three metadata layers."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is it legal to remove metadata from a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes, in virtually all cases. You are modifying your own document's hidden properties. The exception would be deliberately removing metadata from a document you intend to submit as evidence in legal proceedings, which could constitute tampering depending on jurisdiction. For normal professional document sharing, metadata removal is not only legal but recommended under GDPR's data minimisation principle."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the XMP stream and why does it matter for metadata removal?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "XMP (Extensible Metadata Platform) is an XML-based metadata store embedded inside PDF files. Modern PDFs carry metadata in two places: the standard Info dictionary and the XMP stream. Many metadata removal tools only clear the Info dictionary, leaving the XMP stream fully intact. Anyone running ExifTool on the 'cleaned' file can still read all the original metadata. Complete removal requires stripping both stores."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I remove metadata from a PDF on a Mac?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "On Mac you have three options: (1) FileIntel's browser-based PDF Metadata Remover — works on any browser, nothing installed; (2) ExifTool via Terminal: install with 'brew install exiftool' then run 'exiftool -all= document.pdf'; (3) Preview does not remove metadata — it preserves existing metadata and adds its own when you save."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

